          "heading": "Ground models and uncertainty",
          "content": "## Ground models and uncertainty\n\nA foundation site is not a set of soil types stacked like manufactured sheets. Ground originates through geological processes that create lenses, variable density, anisotropic fabrics, relict channels, buried surfaces and occasionally artificial disturbance. This is why investigation begins with a decision rather than with a fixed number of holes. The team needs to know what could alter foundation selection: the depth of competent material, the continuity of soft deposits, groundwater, obstructions, contamination, seismic hazards or neighboring excavations. A borehole section should communicate confidence and alternative interpretations along with the nominal strata.\n\nThe distinction between data and inference is central. A laboratory water content is a measurement, provided the sampling and calibration records are intact. Classifying a layer as continuous between two widely spaced boreholes is an inference. Predicting a pile toe elevation from that inferred layer is a consequential engineering decision. The reader should keep these three levels separate so that uncertainties can be reduced where they matter rather than hidden behind a smooth drawing. A property table alone is never a complete ground model.\n\nInvestigations take place in stages. A preliminary desk study can examine nearby structures, topographic history, groundwater and natural hazards. Reconnaissance tests broad hypotheses about fill, buried channels and accessibility. Detailed testing then focuses on critical load paths. During construction, new ground exposure may test or overturn the predictions. The engineering process therefore includes a feedback mechanism: when observed strata or groundwater diverge from the design model, there must be a defined action pathway. This is especially important for piles whose installed toe or shaft may pass through unexpected layers.\n\nA well-written geotechnical report distinguishes characteristic choices from raw results, explains the methods used for correlations and gives ranges for parameters that dominate settlement or bearing calculations. It does not promise that a sparse investigation eliminates surprises. Instead, it makes risks legible enough to allocate testing, contingency, monitoring and construction controls.\n\n**Case integration: the forgotten embankment.** A proposed library building occupies land that has been levelled repeatedly over several decades. The desk study reveals historic aerial photographs showing a narrow drainage channel crossing the eastern half and a former workshop near the western boundary. Recent boreholes identify alternating fill and alluvium but do not clearly reveal whether the old channel was excavated and backfilled with organic matter. A conventional uniform sand profile would miss this history. Write three ground models: a competent continuous sand lens, a discontinuous sand lens over soft clay, and heterogeneous made ground. These models imply distinct footing settlement risks and potentially different pile installation problems. Assign a consequence to each scenario, then target an additional exploratory line to the critical interface rather than drilling at aesthetically symmetric locations. The investigation plan should include recovered samples, in-situ data and repeated water observations at screened depths. Specify how construction observations will be compared with the interpreted layer levels. The aim is to turn a collection of holes into a falsifiable ground explanation, not to eliminate every possible uncertainty. If a low-probability channel crossing could cause major differential settlement, it deserves explicit attention even when the average laboratory results look favorable."
