          "heading": "Selection, safety cases and verification",
          "content": "## Selection, safety cases and verification\n\nFoundation selection is an engineering decision under uncertainty, not a contest between footing and pile calculators. It should identify governing load cases, predicted settlement ranges, construction access, water conditions, neighboring infrastructure, available materials and consequences of failure. Initial cost is only one dimension. A shallow foundation can be economical in concrete but expensive in groundwater control; piles may avoid deep excavation yet demand noise mitigation and substantial testing. Improvement may be efficient if it can be verified within the schedule.\n\nA transparent decision matrix is useful when its limitations are explicit. Weights and ratings are expressions of stakeholder priorities and project assumptions. If the ranking changes drastically under small adjustments, the selection is fragile and requires further evidence. A defensible recommendation should state the information that would reverse the conclusion, not simply present the highest score as truth. The interactive laboratory illustrates this sensitivity with synthetic ratings intentionally unrelated to market costs.\n\nThe design basis should record the governing standard and its edition, soil and groundwater parameters, calculation conventions, load combinations, serviceability limits, project constraints and approvals. Mixing a resistance reduction factor from one design format with service actions from another can create a false impression of safety. Codes also change; this open textbook does not reproduce proprietary standards or claim to stay automatically current. Project engineers must check their jurisdiction and applicable editions.\n\nVerification connects the report with the constructed work. Construction logs, inspection, test results, monitoring records, nonconformances and remedial actions should be part of a traceable record. Independent review is particularly important when geology is complex or failure consequences are high. A useful handover states both what has been demonstrated and what remains uncertain. The educational labs here can support that conversation, but none can approve a real foundation.\n\n**Case integration: an investment decision with uncertain geology.** A developer compares spread footings, a raft and piles for a medium-rise building on partially investigated ground. The first cost estimate favors shallow foundations, but a possible deep soft layer creates substantial settlement uncertainty. A transparent decision requires the same loads, groundwater state and performance criteria for each alternative. A weighted score can document priorities such as cost, serviceability and schedule, but synthetic ratings must never be mistaken for market estimates or geotechnical evidence. Carry at least two ground scenarios through all alternatives and compute a range of plausible outcomes. Identify the price of a targeted site investigation and compare it with the potential cost of remediation or a design change if the soft layer exists. A preferred option is defensible only if it remains acceptable under the agreed credible scenarios or if a verification step is scheduled to resolve a critical unknown. The final design record should include rejected options with reasons, versioned assumptions, the adopted code basis and a clear handoff to construction testing and settlement monitoring."
